    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "3D seismic surveys provide a means of capturing 3D details of fault systems such as tipline shapes, slip distributions and segmentation effects. A 3D seismic dataset from an oilfied in southern England was used to determine normal fault characteristics. Seismic attribute maps capture subtle changes in fault geometry, such as segmentation features. Gently curving faults exhibit numerous E-W linear features along the fault trace in dip magnitude maps that imply a segmented fault architecture. The E-W orientation is consistent with the N-S orientation of the Atlantic opening principal extension axis. Major faults thus developed through a process of segment linkage. Initial linkage in the deepest portions of the faulted stratigraphy progressed upsection in response to the upwards propagation of individual fault segments. Major faults in the survey form a systematic patchwork geometry when viewed either perpendicular to fault strike or from directly above, irrespective of fault dips and spatial association. Individual faults exhibit a variety of shapes that are dependent on the shape of the available gap in the patchwork array. This organization of fault geometry suggests that the presence of strain shadow zones around faults may be the primary control on the locations of adjacent faults as well as their tipline shapes.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
